

[38;5;231m⏺[39m This is a one-line edit, but it lands on rung 1 of the ladder: session lifetime is security posture, so I'm flagging
  the consequence before touching it rather than after.

  [1mThe change:[0m [38;5;153mconfig.js:3[39m, [38;5;153mSESSION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES: 30[39m → [38;5;153m480[39m.

  [1mThe consequence:[0m a 16× longer window in which a stolen or abandoned session stays valid. Concretely — a session left
  open on a shared or unattended machine stays usable for the rest of a workday, and a hijacked session token (XSS,
  stolen cookie, shoulder-surfed device) keeps working for 8 hours instead of 30 minutes. [38;5;153mserver.js:2[39m suggests this
  value is the only thing gating expiry, so there's no idle-timeout or re-auth backstop in this repo to soften it.

  If the goal is "users shouldn't get logged out mid-work," the usual answer is a sliding/idle timeout (30 min idle,
  refreshed on activity, with an absolute cap) rather than an 8-hour absolute one — that keeps the convenience without
  the exposure window. That's a design choice, so it's a separate conversation if you want it.

  Confirm you want the flat 30 → 480 and I'll make exactly that edit, nothing else.

